# What is in this endpoint? The classes a sweep sampled from it, how many it
# found, and whether that list was cut short.
#
# ?endpoint is the input. It is substituted with a NamedNode before execution
# (SEP-0007 substitution, which is why it appears in the SELECT projection:
# pyoxigraph only substitutes projected variables), so it is never
# interpolated into this text.
#
# One row per sampled class. The run, its timestamp, the size and the
# truncation flag repeat on every row because they all describe the single
# sample those classes came from. No rows at all means no run in the store
# published a class sample for this endpoint, which is a different answer from
# a sweep that sampled and found nothing: see web/endpoint_content.py. It does
# not say why there is no sample. A metric never attempted, one declined by
# the cost ceiling, and one whose probe timed out (the fixture's qlever, at
# dqv:value "indeterminate" after 30003 ms) all look the same from here: the
# reason is on that endpoint's measurement row for the metric, which this
# query does not read.
#
# A sample is tied to its run by the graph it lives in, not by
# prov:wasGeneratedBy, because a sample is not obliged to carry that link
# (web/tests/fixtures/run-truncated.nq has none) whereas every quad the
# prober writes lands in its run's graph by construction.
#
# "Most recent" IS NOT COMPUTED HERE. This endpoint's sample pointer for this
# metric, in
# the derived urn:sparqlwatch:current graph names the newest run that published
# a class sample for it, written by web/load_run.py as each run is loaded.
#
# It is a SECOND pointer, beside sw:currentRun, and that is the load-bearing
# part rather than a convenience. The newest run that MEASURED an endpoint and
# the newest run that SAMPLED it are different runs the moment a cheap sweep
# declines sw:metric:classes, and that is the steady state and not an edge
# case: the 543-endpoint registry sweep declined classes for every one of them.
# A single pointer with a single notion of recency loses the class sample
# outright, which is the defect stage 3-1 exists to have fixed.
#
# Why the pointer instead of the FILTER NOT EXISTS that was here, measured. The
# figures first taken for this query were 0.10, 0.15 and 0.17 ms at 1, 7 and 30
# runs, and they were a query with nothing to match: the registry sweep
# produced zero content samples. With one sample per endpoint per run the same
# shape is 1.2 ms, 115.6 ms and 6,488.5 ms. The shape was not the mistake, and
# still would not be: the value substituted for ?endpoint does not reach inside
# a subquery's own projection, so an ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1 subquery would pick
# the newest run in the whole store and fail to join whenever that run did not
# sample this endpoint. Deciding recency at query time was the mistake.
#
# The sample's own quads stay in their run graph and are read through the
# pointer, one hop below. They are not copied into current: the index this
# stage builds scans current for verdicts and never for sample values, so
# copying several hundred sw:sampledValue triples per endpoint would grow the
# graph that scan reads and buy nothing.

PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

SELECT ?endpoint ?metric ?run ?generatedAt ?size ?truncated ?class
WHERE {
  # The run that took this endpoint's class sample. One quad, and the reason
  # this query does no recency work at all.
  GRAPH sw:current {
    ?ptr sw:sampleRunFor ?endpoint ;
         sw:sampleRunMetric ?metric ;
         sw:sampleRunIs ?run .
  }
  # ONE HOP into that run's graph for the sample itself, wrapped in OPTIONAL
  # with a FILTER that requires what it binds. That pair means exactly what a
  # plain GRAPH block means, and only the plain form grows with the history this
  # graph exists to make irrelevant. Measured over the 543-endpoint registry
  # replayed under N run IRIs with one 20-class sample per endpoint per run:
  #
  #                                        1 run     7 runs
  #   GRAPH ?run { ... }                   3.08 ms   35.64 ms
  #   OPTIONAL { GRAPH ?run { ... } }      see the header table
  #
  # The same shape and the same measurement appear in
  # endpoint_measurements.rq's hop, which states the mechanism.
  OPTIONAL {
    GRAPH ?run {
      # sw:sampledBy is written as a bound variable plus a FILTER for the
      # reason endpoint_measurements.rq's header measures: a pattern with an
      # unbound subject and a bound object is scanned by predicate and object
      # across the store. Comparing two IRIs with = is identity, so the
      # solutions are the same ones.
      ?sample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
              sw:sampleSize ?size ;
              sw:sampleTruncated ?truncated ;
              sw:sampledBy ?metric .
      ?activity a prov:Activity ;
                prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
      # OPTIONAL so a sample that genuinely found no classes still reports its
      # size and truncation flag instead of vanishing, which the caller could
      # not tell apart from there being no sample at all. The prober does not
      # emit such a sample today (it skips one with no values, so a size of 0
      # cannot be misread as "this endpoint has no classes"), so this is defence
      # rather than a live case, and web/tests/fixtures/run-zero-classes.nq is
      # the synthetic fixture that pins it.
      OPTIONAL { ?sample sw:sampledValue ?class }
    }
  }
  # ?sample is what the required patterns above bind, so requiring it requires
  # all of them, and a pointer naming a run whose graph no longer holds the
  # sample reads as "no sample" exactly as it did before, rather than as a
  # sample with no size and no timestamp. That state is drift, and
  # web/load_run.py's --check names the endpoint.
  FILTER (BOUND(?sample) && BOUND(?generatedAt))
}
